message: "If you use or reference this work, please cite it as below."
title: "Strategic Forgetting: Ephemeral Retrieval and Tiered Context Persistence for LLM Inference"
type: report
authors:
  - family-names: Phan
    given-names: Ivan
    alias: HiP
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-1095-5855"
date-released: "2026-03-17"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.19212126"
url: "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.19212126"
license: CC-BY-4.0
abstract: >-
  Tool-augmented LLM sessions accumulate retrieved content that actively
  degrades the model's ability to attend to relevant material, not merely
  wasting context capacity but reducing output quality through documented
  attention degradation. This concept note proposes a tiered retention
  architecture with three modes (full, summary, ephemeral), four
  recoverability classes, user-settable priority, and a retention note
  schema that makes every compression decision inspectable. The security
  analysis identifies semantic cache poisoning as a specific risk when
  summarisation is performed on untrusted content. The paper specifies
  the retention contract; calibrating defaults by content type, task type,
  and user profile is a platform-level product decision.
keywords:
  - context management
  - LLM inference
  - tiered retention
  - ephemeral retrieval
  - context window
  - tool-augmented AI
  - prompt injection
  - semantic cache poisoning
  - inspectability
  - AI safety
  - summarisation security
  - retention architecture
